<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mason–Dixon line</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1763">1763</a>–<a href="/years/1767">1767</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mason–Dixon line is a boundary surveyed between 1763 and 1767 by Charles Mason and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a> Dixon to settle a long dispute between the proprietors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>. It fixed the borders between Pennsylvania, Maryland and Delaware, and in the 19th century it came to symbolise the division between the free North and the slave-holding South.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mason-dixon-line.jpg" alt="Mason–Dixon line" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States Department of the Interior · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mason-dixon-line.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Mason–Dixon line is made up of four segments that follow the terms of the settlement of 1767: the tangent line, the north line, the arc line and the parallel at 39°43′ N. The east–west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> boundary lies 15 miles south of what was then the southernmost point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, and the surveyors also fixed the arc that bounds Delaware around New Castle and the boundary between Delaware and Maryland. The line was marked by stones every mile and by crownstones every five miles, shipped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, with an M on the Maryland side and a P on the Pennsylvania side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>'s charter of 1632 granted land north of the Potomac up to the 40th parallel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>'s charter of 1681 defined its southern border as the same line but described it differently, because Charles II relied on an inaccurate map. The 40th parallel lies north of the site Penn had chosen for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, so the Maryland claim would have put the city in Maryland. A provisional agreement of 1732 between Lord Baltimore and Penn's sons was never carried out, and from the mid-1730s settlers loyal to each colony fought in what became known as Cresap's War. The dispute ended when Frederick Calvert, 6th Baron Baltimore, accepted the earlier agreements.</p><p>The Penns and the Calverts then commissioned the English astronomer Charles Mason and the surveyor Jeremiah Dixon, who worked between 1763 and 1767 and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> line in April 1765. It cost £3,512 and covered 244 miles. In 1767 their Iroquois guides refused to go further, having reached the edge of their lands, where they were at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>, and in 1784 David Rittenhouse and Andrew Ellicott carried the line to the southwest corner of Pennsylvania. A small wedge of land between Delaware and Pennsylvania stayed in dispute until 1921.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 1768 official report of the survey did not mention Mason and Dixon by name. The phrase 'Mason and Dixon's line' came into wide use in the congressional debates on the Missouri Compromise of 1820, because after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1781 the line and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a> formed a border between slave and free states. Delaware kept slavery until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a> in 1865. The Confederate States of America claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> portion of the line as part of its northern border. The name is still used for the line dividing the Northeast from the South.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mason–Dixon line” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Mason-Dixon line</b>, surveyed from 1763, settled the long border dispute with the Province of Pennsylvania.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-maryland">Province of Maryland</a></li></ul></section>
